\(y=c\\);\n* vertical: \\(x=c\\);\n* slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nAmong the six points, the only such lines that contain at least two points are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&x=1 &&\\text{contains }A,B,C,\\\\\n&x=2 &&\\text{contains }D,E,\\\\\n&y=1 &&\\text{contains }A,D,F,\\\\\n&y=2 &&\\text{contains }B,E,\\\\\n&x+y=3 &&\\text{contains }B,D,\\\\\n&x+y=4 &&\\text{contains }C,E,F .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nWe must choose one of the lines in (1) to be the unique non\u2011sunny line (the other two lines must be sunny).  \nWe examine all possibilities.\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Non\u2011sunny line contains three points\n\n*If the non\u2011sunny line is \\(x=1\\) (contains \\(A,B,C\\)).*  \nThe remaining points are \\(D,E,F\\). Their pairwise slopes are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{slope}(D,E)&=\\infty\\;( \\text{vertical}),\\\\\n\\operatorname{slope}(D,F)&=0\\;( \\text{horizontal}),\\\\\n\\operatorname{slope}(E,F)&=-1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll three are forbidden, so no sunny line can pass through two of \\(\\{D,E,F\\}\\).  \nWith only two sunny lines available we could cover at most two of these points, leaving one uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.\n\n*If the non\u2011sunny line is \\(y=1\\) (contains \\(A,D,F\\)).*  \nThe remaining points are \\(B,C,E\\). Their pairwise slopes are  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{slope}(B,C)=\\infty,\\;\n\\operatorname{slope}(B,E)=0,\\;\n\\operatorname{slope}(C,E)=-1,\n\\]\n\nagain all forbidden. The same contradiction follows.\n\n*If the non\u2011sunny line is \\(x+y=4\\) (contains \\(C,E,F\\)).*  \nThe remaining points are \\(A,B,D\\) with slopes  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{slope}(A,B)=\\infty,\\;\n\\operatorname{slope}(A,D)=0,\\;\n\\operatorname{slope}(B,D)=-1,\n\\]\n\nagain impossible.\n\nHence a non\u2011sunny line covering three points cannot lead to a valid configuration.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Non\u2011sunny line contains exactly two points\n\nWe consider each line in (1) that contains exactly two points.\n\n*Case \\(y=2\\) (contains \\(B,E\\)).*  \nThe four uncovered points are \\(A,C,D,F\\). Their pairwise slopes are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{slope}(A,C)&=\\infty,\\\\\n\\operatorname{slope}(A,D)&=0,\\\\\n\\operatorname{slope}(A,F)&=0,\\\\\n\\operatorname{slope}(C,D)&=-2\\;(\\text{sunny}),\\\\\n\\operatorname{slope}(C,F)&=-1,\\\\\n\\operatorname{slope}(D,F)&=0 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nOnly the pair \\((C,D)\\) yields a sunny line; the remaining two points \\(A\\) and \\(F\\) lie on a horizontal line, which is not sunny. Thus the two sunny lines cannot cover all four points.\n\n*Case \\(x=2\\) (contains \\(D,E\\)).*  \nUncovered points: \\(A,B,C,F\\). Their slopes are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{slope}(A,B)&=\\infty,\\;\n\\operatorname{slope}(A,C)&=\\infty,\\;\n\\operatorname{slope}(A,F)&=0,\\\\\n\\operatorname{slope}(B,C)&=\\infty,\\;\n\\operatorname{slope}(B,F)&=-\\tfrac12\\;(\\text{sunny}),\\\\\n\\operatorname{slope}(C,F)&=-1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nOnly \\((B,F)\\) gives a sunny line; the remaining points \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) are vertical, not sunny. Again impossible.\n\nThe remaining possibilities \\(x=1\\), \\(x+y=3\\) are symmetric to the above and lead to the same obstruction.\n\nThus no choice of a non\u2011sunny line covering exactly two points allows the two remaining sunny lines to cover the other four points.\n\n---\n\nSince every possible placement of the single non\u2011sunny line leads to a contradiction, there is **no** arrangement of three distinct lines with exactly two sunny lines that covers all required points for \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, a configuration with }k=2\\text{ sunny lines does not exist.}}\n\\]"
