 \\(\\mathcal L\\) consisting of \\(n\\) distinct lines and that among them there is at least one vertical line.  \n> Pick a vertical line \\(x=p\\) belonging to \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \n> Because the point \\((p,1)\\) belongs to \\(S_n\\) (since \\(p+1\\le n+1\\)), there exists at least one integer \\(q\\) with \\(1\\le q\\le n+1-p\\); choose any such \\(q\\).”\n\n*Verification:*  \n- If a vertical line \\(x=p\\) is present, then \\(p\\) is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\).  \n- Since \\(p+1\\le n+1\\), the point \\((p,1)\\) is indeed in \\(S_n\\).  \n- Consequently there exists at least one integer \\(q\\) satisfying \\(1\\le q\\le n+1-p\\).  \nAll statements are valid. **No issue.**\n\n> **Quote:** “Now fix a real number  \n> \\[\n> m\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\n> \\tag{5}\n> \\]  \n> and consider the line  \n> \\[\n> \\ell_{p,q}: \\; y-q = m\\,(x-p).\n> \\tag{6}\n> \\]  \n> Because \\(m\\neq 0,-1\\), \\(\\ell_{p,q}\\) is sunny. Moreover \\(\\ell_{p,q}\\) passes through \\((p,q)\\).”\n\n*Verification:*  \n- The line defined by (6) indeed has slope \\(m\\) and passes through \\((p,q)\\).  \n- Since \\(m\\neq0\\) (non‑horizontal) and \\(m\\neq-1\\) (non‑parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)), and \\(m\\) is finite (non‑vertical), the line is sunny by definition.  \nAll statements are correct. **No issue.**\n\n---\n\n### Section 2 – Choice of Slope and Claim About the Second Lattice Intersection\n\n> **Quote:** “Choose \\(m\\) so that the second intersection of \\(\\ell_{p,q}\\) with the integer lattice lies inside \\(S_n\\); a convenient choice is  \n> \\[\n> m = -\\frac{1}{2},\n> \\tag{7}\n> \\]  \n> which yields the point \\((p+2,q-1)\\).”\n\n*Verification:*  \n- For \\(m=-\\frac12\\), substituting \\(x=p+2\\) gives \\(y-q = -\\frac12\\cdot2 = -1\\), so \\(y = q-1\\). Hence \\((p+2,q-1)\\) is indeed an integer point on the line.  \n- The existence of this second integer point is correct.\n\n> **Quote:** “Since \\(q\\le n+1-p\\), we have  \n> \\[\n> (p+2)+(q-1)=p+q+1\\le n+2,\n> \\]  \n> and because \\(p\\ge 1\\) we obtain \\((p+2)+(q-1)\\le n+1\\) for all admissible \\(p,q\\) except the extreme case \\(p=n\\) (where the vertical line will never be swapped).”\n\n*Verification:*  \n- From \\(q\\le n+1-p\\) we deduce \\(p+q\\le n+1\\). Adding 1 yields \\(p+q+1\\le n+2\\). This part is correct.  \n- The next claim, “because \\(p\\ge1\\) we obtain \\((p+2)+(q-1)\\le n+1\\)”, is **false**. The inequality \\((p+2)+(q-1)\\le n+1\\) is equivalent to \\(p+q\\le n\\). However, we only know \\(p+q\\le n+1\\); the case \\(p+q=n+1\\) is allowed and then \\((p+2)+(q-1)=n+2>n+1\\). Thus the point \\((p+2,q-1)\\) does **not** always belong to \\(S_n\\). The argument that it does for all admissible \\((p,q)\\) (except \\(p=n\\)) is incorrect.  \n**Classification:** **Critical Error** (factual mistake that invalidates the subsequent covering argument).\n\n---\n\n### Section 2 – Replacement of the Vertical Line\n\n> **Quote:** “Now replace the vertical line \\(x=p\\) in \\(\\mathcal L\\) by the sunny line \\(\\ell_{p,q}\\).  \n> All points of \\(S_n\\) that were covered by \\(x=p\\) remain covered because \\((p,q)\\) is still on \\(\\ell_{p,q}\\); the other points of the former vertical line are covered by the horizontal line \\(y=q\\), which is already present in \\(\\mathcal L\\) (if it is not, we may first add it – this does not change the total number of lines because we will later delete another vertical line).”\n\n*Verification:*  \n- The vertical line \\(x=p\\) originally covered **all** points \\((p,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-p\\). After removal, only the single point \\((p,q)\\) remains covered by \\(\\ell_{p,q}\\).  \n- The claim that “the other points … are covered by the horizontal line \\(y=q\\)” is **incorrect**: a horizontal line \\(y=q\\) contains points \\((a,q)\\) for all \\(a\\), but it does **not** contain points \\((p,b)\\) with \\(b\\neq q\\). Hence those points become uncovered.  \n- Moreover, in the initial covering \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) there are **no** horizontal lines. Adding a horizontal line would increase the total number of lines beyond \\(n\\); the subsequent statement that “this does not change the total number of lines because we will later delete another vertical line” is not a valid justification because the deletion of a vertical line would again remove coverage of its column of points, re‑introducing uncovered points.  \nThus the replacement does **not** preserve the covering property, nor does it guarantee that the total number of lines stays \\(n\\).  \n**Classification:** **Critical Error** (logical flaw that breaks the core construction).\n\n---\n\n### Section 2 – Stated Consequences of the Replacement\n\n> **Quote:** “Consequences of the replacement:  \n> * the number of lines stays \\(n\\);  \n> * the number of sunny lines increases by exactly 1;  \n> * the family of lines remains a covering of \\(S_n\\).”\n\n*Verification:*  \n- As shown above, the replacement does **not** keep the covering intact, and the line count may change. Therefore none of the three listed consequences is justified.  \n**Classification:** **Critical Error** (directly follows from the previous flawed step).\n\n---\n\n### Section 3 – Iterative Construction of Any \\(k\\)\n\n> **Quote:** “Start from the all‑vertical covering \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) (which has \\(0\\) sunny lines).  \n> Perform the swap described above successively on distinct vertical lines \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots ,x=k\\) (any order).  \n> After the \\(i\\)-th swap we have a covering consisting of  \n> * \\(i\\) sunny lines,  \n> * \\(n-i\\) vertical lines,  \n> * possibly some horizontal lines that were used as anchors; they are never removed, so the total number of lines is always exactly \\(n\\).”\n\n*Verification:*  \n- This entire paragraph relies on the “swap” operation whose validity has been disproved. Since the swap does not preserve the covering nor the line count, the iterative construction cannot be guaranteed to produce a valid covering with the claimed number of sunny lines.  \n- The statement that “horizontal lines … are never removed, so the total number of lines is always exactly \\(n\\)” contradicts the earlier claim that the total number of lines stays \\(n\\) after each swap; both cannot hold simultaneously given the flawed swap.  \n**Classification:** **Critical Error** (the argument is built on an invalid premise).\n\n---\n\n### Section 3 – Claim About Realising Every \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots,n\\}\\)\n\n> **Quote:** “Since a swap can be performed as long as at least one vertical line remains, we can realise every integer  \n> \\[\n> k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n> \\tag{8}\n> \\]”\n\n*Verification:*  \n- Because the swap operation is invalid, the ability to perform it “as long as at least one vertical line remains” is not established. Consequently, the claim that every integer \\(k\\) in the range can be realized lacks proof.  \n**Classification:** **Critical Error** (conclusion unsupported).\n\n---\n\n### Section 4 – Upper Bound\n\n> **Quote:** “The definition of the problem requires exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines. Consequently the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).”\n\n*Verification:*  \n- This observation is trivially correct: with \\(n\\) distinct lines, at most \\(n\\) of them can be sunny. No issue.\n\n---\n\n### Section 5 – Final Conclusion\n\n> **Quote:** “For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly the integers between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\), inclusive.  \n> \\[\n> \\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n\\,\\}}\n> \\]”\n\n*Verification:*  \n- The conclusion depends on the construction claimed in Sections 2–3. Since those constructions contain critical errors, the conclusion is **not** established. The solution fails to demonstrate the existence of coverings with any prescribed \\(k>0\\). Therefore the final answer is unsupported.\n\n---\n\n### Overall Assessment\n\nThe solution correctly identifies a trivial covering with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines. However, the central “swap” operation intended to increase the number of sunny lines is fundamentally flawed:\n\n1. The claim that the second lattice point \\((p+2,q-1)\\) always lies in \\(S_n\\) is false.\n2. Replacing a vertical line by a single sunny line leaves most points of that column uncovered; the proposed remedy using a horizontal line \\(y=q\\) does not cover those points and introduces an unjustified change in the total number of lines.\n3. Consequently, the asserted preservation of the covering and the constancy of the line count after each swap are invalid.\n4. All subsequent arguments that rely on the swap (iterative construction, realization of any \\(k\\), final answer) collapse.\n\nBecause these errors break the logical chain of the proof, the solution does **not** constitute a valid solution to the problem."
