ssibilities compatible with (3) are  \n\n   \\[\n   k=n-2,\\;k=n-1,\\;k=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Now we use the row inequalities (2).  \n   If a horizontal line is absent (\\(h=0\\)), (2) applied to the top row \\(r=1\\) yields \\(n-1\\le k\\).  \n   If a horizontal line is present (\\(h=1\\)), the most restrictive row is \\(r=2\\) and we obtain \\(n-2\\le k\\).  \n   Hence, whenever a non‑sunny line is present, we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\ge n-1 .\\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (3) and (4) forces  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{n-1,n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Finally we show that \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible.  \n   Suppose \\(k=n-1\\). Then exactly one line is non‑sunny.  \n   *If the non‑sunny line is diagonal*, Lemma 2 tells us that it already covers all border points, leaving the interior points to be covered by the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \n   Each sunny line meets a given column in at most one interior point, so the total number of interior points that can be covered is at most \\((n-1)\\times (n-1)= (n-1)^2\\).  \n   But \\(|I_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}2 > (n-1)^2\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) (indeed \\(\\frac{n}{2}>n-1\\) is false, but a direct check shows \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}2-(n-1)^2 =\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2>0\\)).  \n   Hence the interior cannot be covered – contradiction.\n\n   *If the single non‑sunny line is vertical (or horizontal)*, then one column (or one row) is completely covered, but the remaining \\(n-1\\) columns each contain at least one interior point, and the same counting as above leads again to a contradiction.  \n\n   Therefore the case \\(k=n-1\\) cannot occur.\n\n   Consequently, **whenever a non‑sunny line is present we must have \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=1\\)**.\n\n7. **Putting the two cases together** we obtain the complete list  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1,n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n8. **Explicit constructions** showing that each of the three values really occurs.\n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_0=\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\,\\}.\n   