ist of possible values of \\(k\\):\n   \\[\n   k=0,\\qquad k=1,\\qquad k=n .\n   \\]\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Preliminary lemmas  \n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nIf a line \\(L\\) is sunny then for every integer \\(c\\)\n\n* \\(L\\) meets the vertical line \\(x=c\\) in at most one point,\n* \\(L\\) meets the horizontal line \\(y=c\\) in at most one point,\n* \\(L\\) meets the anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  A sunny line has a well‑defined finite slope different from \\(-1\\); therefore the equations\n\\(x=c\\), \\(y=c\\) and \\(x+y=c\\) each give a linear equation in the remaining variable, which has at most one solution. ∎\n\n\n\n**Lemma 2.**  \nA non‑sunny line is either\n\n* vertical: \\(x=c\\);\n* horizontal: \\(y=c\\);\n* diagonal of slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nSuch a line meets a given column (resp. row, anti‑diagonal) either in **all** its points (the vertical, horizontal, or the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\)) or in at most one point. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Three explicit families\n\n#### 2.1  No sunny line (\\(k=0\\))\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\,\\}\n\\]\n\nare all non‑sunny and clearly cover every point of \\(T_n\\) because each point has abscissa between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\).  Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  All lines sunny (\\(k=n\\))\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;y-(n+1-i)=-\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,(x-i). \\tag{★}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(-\\dfrac{i}{i+1}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny, and the slopes are distinct, so the lines are distinct.\n\nFix a column \\(i\\).  Substituting \\(x=i\\) in (★) gives \\(y=n+1-i\\); decreasing the abscissa by one changes the ordinate by \\(\\dfrac{i}{i+1}\\), which is never an integer.  Consequently the only integer points of \\(\\ell_i\\) with abscissa \\(\\le i\\) are precisely the points \\((i,b)\\) with  \n\n\\[\n1\\le b\\le n+1-i .\n\\]\n\nThese are exactly the points of \\(T_n\\) that lie in column \\(i\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to the line indexed by its first coordinate, and all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  Thus \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Exactly one sunny line (\\(k=1\\))\n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n* \\(L_0:\\;y=x\\) (sunny, slope \\(1\\));\n* \\(D:\\;x+y=n+1\\) (diagonal, non‑sunny);\n* \\(H:\\;y=1\\) (horizontal, non‑sunny);\n* for each integer \\(a\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le n-2\\) (the set is empty when \\(n=3\\)) the vertical line  \n\n  \\[\n  V_a:\\;x=a .\n  \\]\n\nThe family contains exactly one sunny line (\\(L_0\\)) and \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) belongs to one of the following groups, and each group is covered by the indicated line:\n\n* **\\(a=1\\).**  The point lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\); when \\(n\\ge4\\) this line is \\(V_1\\), and when \\(n=3\\) it coincides with the diagonal \\(D\\).\n* **\\(b=1\\).**  The point lies on the horizontal line \\(H\\).\n* **\\(a+b=n+1\\).**  The point lies on the diagonal \\(D\\).\n* **\\(a=b\\).**  The point lies on the sunny line \\(L_0\\).\n* **\\(2\\le a\\le n-2\\) and \\(a\\neq b\\).**  The point lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\nThus all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered and exactly one line is sunny.  Hence \\(k=1\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  No other value of \\(k\\) is possible\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_n\\) and that at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is non‑sunny.  Let  \n\n\\[\ns=\\#\\{\\text{sunny lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\nv=\\#\\{\\text{vertical lines in }\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause two different vertical lines have different abscissae, \\(v\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  A vertical line is unavoidable  \n\nIf \\(v=0\\) then column 1 (which contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,b),\\;1\\le b\\le n\\)) must be covered only by sunny lines and by non‑vertical non‑sunny lines (horizontal or diagonal).  By Lemma 1 each sunny line meets column 1 in at most one point, and by Lemma 2 each non‑vertical non‑sunny line also meets column 1 in at most one point.  Hence at least \\(n\\) distinct lines are required to cover column 1, forcing \\(s=n\\) and contradicting the presence of a non‑sunny line.  Therefore **any feasible configuration that contains a non‑sunny line must contain at least one vertical line**; i.e. \\(v\\ge1\\).\n\nFix one vertical line of \\(\\mathcal L\\); denote its abscissa by \\(c\\) and delete it from the family.  The remaining \\(n-1\\) lines have to cover the points of the other \\(n-1\\) columns.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  Bounding the number of sunny lines  \n\nConsider a column \\(j\\neq c\\).  In this column the deleted vertical line is absent, so each of the remaining lines contributes at most one point (Lemma 1 for sunny lines, Lemma 2 for the non‑vertical non‑sunny lines).  Consequently the \\(s\\) sunny lines can supply at most \\(s\\) points of column \\(j\\).  Column \\(j\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-j\\) points; therefore at least  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(n+1-j\\bigr)-s\\quad\\text{(if positive)}\n\\]\n\npoints of this column must be supplied by the non‑vertical non‑sunny lines.\n\nSumming over all columns \\(j\\neq c\\) we obtain a lower bound for the number of non‑vertical non‑sunny lines that are needed:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j\\neq c}\\max\\bigl\\{0,\\,(n+1-j)-s\\bigr\\}\\;\\le\\;(n-s)-1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe right‑hand side equals the number of available non‑vertical non‑sunny lines (the total number of non‑sunny lines is \\(n-s\\); one of them has already been removed as the vertical line).\n\nNow compute the left‑hand side.  Because the columns are ordered by decreasing size, the largest term occurs for the smallest index different from \\(c\\).  If \\(c\\neq1\\) the first term is \\((n-1)-s\\); if \\(c=1\\) the first term is \\((n-2)-s\\).  In either case, for every integer \\(s\\ge2\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)-s\\ge n-3\\ge1 .\n\\]\n\nAdding the contributions of the remaining columns (each of which is non‑negative) yields a sum **strictly larger** than \\(n-s-1\\) whenever \\(s\\ge2\\).  Hence inequality (1) cannot hold for \\(s\\ge2\\).\n\nTherefore **\\(s\\le1\\)**; i.e. a covering that contains a non‑sunny line can have at most one sunny line.  Consequently the only possible values of \\(k\\) in this situation are \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.3  The case “all lines sunny”\n\nIf no line of the family is non‑sunny, then every line is sunny, so \\(k=n\\).  The construction of Section 2.2 shows that this value is indeed attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nThe analysis yields exactly three admissible numbers of sunny lines:\n\n* \\(k=0\\) – realised by the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\);\n* \\(k=1\\) – realised by the family described in Section 2.3;\n* \\(k=n\\) – realised by the family described in Section 2.2.\n\nNo other integer \\(k\\) can occur.  Hence the answer to the problem is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,\\,1,\\,n\\}} .\n\\]"
