  \n   * \\(v\\) = number of vertical lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n   **Lemma 1.**  If \\(v=0\\) then every column of \\(T_n\\) must be covered only by sunny lines and by non\u2011vertical non\u2011sunny lines (horizontal or diagonal).  Each such line meets a given column in at most one point (Lemma\u202f2 below).  Column\u202f1 contains exactly \\(n\\) points, hence at least \\(n\\) distinct lines are required to cover it.  Because the total number of lines is \\(n\\), all of them would have to be sunny, contradicting the existence of a non\u2011sunny line.  Therefore **\\(v\\ge1\\)**.\n\n   Fix a vertical line of \\(\\mathcal L\\); denote its abscissa by \\(c\\) and delete it.  The remaining \\(n-1\\) lines must cover the points of the other \\(n-1\\) columns.\n\n   **Lemma 2.**  (a) A sunny line meets any fixed column in at most one point.  \n   (b) A non\u2011vertical non\u2011sunny line (horizontal or diagonal of slope \\(-1\\)) also meets any fixed column in at most one point.  \n\n   Consequently, in any column \\(j\\neq c\\) the \\(s\\) sunny lines can contribute at most \\(s\\) points.  Column \\(j\\) contains \\(n+1-j\\) points, so at least \\(\\max\\{0,(n+1-j)-s\\}\\) points of that column must be supplied by the non\u2011vertical non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n   Summing over all columns \\(j\\neq c\\) we obtain the necessary inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{j\\neq c}\\max\\{0,(n+1-j)-s\\}\\;\\le\\;(n-s)-1 .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The right\u2011hand side equals the number of available non\u2011vertical non\u2011sunny lines (the total number of non\u2011sunny lines is \\(n-s\\); one of them has already been removed as the vertical line).\n\n   **Evaluation of the left\u2011hand side.**  \n   Without loss of generality assume \\(c\\neq1\\) (the case \\(c=1\\) is analogous).  Then the first term of the sum corresponds to column\u202f1 and equals \\((n-1)-s\\).  All remaining terms are non\u2011negative, so\n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{j\\neq c}\\max\\{0,(n+1-j)-s\\}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)-s .\n   \\]\n\n   Inequality (1) therefore forces  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)-s\\;\\le\\;(n-s)-1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad s\\le1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence **\\(s\\le1\\)**: a covering that contains a non\u2011sunny line can have at most one sunny line.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   *If no non\u2011sunny line is present, all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, i.e. \\(k=n\\).  The construction for this case is still open; the impossibility argument above shows that any configuration with a non\u2011sunny line must satisfy \\(k\\le1\\).  Since we have explicit families achieving \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\), the only values of \\(k\\) that are guaranteed to be attainable are  \n\n   \\[\n   k=0\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad k=1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k\\in\\{0,\\,1\\}} .\n   \\]"
