that in any column \\(a\\) the points can be covered by at most one point per line, so if a column contains \\(c\\) points we need at least \\(c\\) lines that intersect that column.  This leads to the inequality  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge \\max_{1\\le a\\le n}\\bigl(n+1-a\\bigr)=n,\n\\]\n\nwhich is clearly impossible for \\(k<n\\).  The flaw in this argument is that a single line may intersect a column in **more than one** point when the line is vertical; the same column can also be intersected by a horizontal or diagonal line in at most one point each.  A correct analysis therefore has to keep track of how many vertical, horizontal and diagonal lines are present and how they share points with the sunny lines.\n\nI attempted to formalise this by letting  \n\n* \\(v\\) = number of vertical lines,\n* \\(h\\) = number of horizontal lines,\n* \\(d\\) = number of diagonal lines (slope \\(-1\\)),\n* \\(s\\) = number of sunny lines,\n\nwith \\(v+h+d+s=n\\).  Each column \\(a\\) contains \\(n+1-a\\) points.  The vertical line \\(x=a\\) (if present) covers the whole column; otherwise the column must be covered by at most one point from each of the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines.  This yields a family of inequalities that any feasible \\((v,h,d,s)\\) must satisfy.  Solving these inequalities for general \\(n\\) turned out to be non‑trivial; the resulting feasible region for \\(s\\) depends on delicate parity considerations and on the exact placement of the non‑sunny lines.\n\nI was able to produce explicit constructions for a few small values of \\(n\\) (e.g. \\(n=3,4\\)) showing that \\(k=0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\) can occur in those cases, but the patterns do not extend straightforwardly to arbitrary \\(n\\).  Conversely, I could not find a convincing impossibility proof that would rule out, say, \\(k\\ge 2\\) for all large \\(n\\).\n\nBecause the core combinatorial optimisation problem remains unresolved, I cannot give a complete answer to the original question.  Further work would be required to