            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **non‑sunny** if it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Reduction to a covering problem\n\nAssume that we have \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\mathcal L\\) such that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nv=\\#\\{\\text{vertical lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\nh=\\#\\{\\text{horizontal lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\n\\]\n\\[\nd=\\#\\{\\text{diagonal lines }x+y=\\text{const in }\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\ns=\\#\\{\\text{sunny lines in }\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\]\n\nClearly  \n\n\\[\nv+h+d+s=n. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIf a covering of \\(T_n\\) exists with at most \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, we may add any \\(k\\) further sunny lines (they may be completely redundant) and obtain a configuration satisfying the statement of the problem with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nHence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\mid T_n\\text{ can be covered by }\\;n-k\\text{ non‑sunny lines}\\,\\}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus we have to determine the smallest possible number of non‑sunny lines needed to cover \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A lower bound for the number of non‑sunny lines\n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that there exists a covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines with  \n\n\\[\nm<n. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) be the set of column indices of the vertical lines that appear in the covering; put \\(|C|=c\\).  \nIf \\(C=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) then we would already have \\(c=n\\ge m\\), contradicting (4).  \nHence \\(C\\neq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and we can define  \n\n\\[\na:=\\min\\{i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\mid i\\notin C\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus the columns \\(1,2,\\dots ,a-1\\) are present among the vertical lines, while column \\(a\\) is absent.\n\nThe column \\(a\\) contains exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:=n+1-a\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\) (namely \\((a,1),(a,2),\\dots ,(a,\\ell)\\)).  \nBecause column \\(a\\) is not represented by a vertical line, none of these \\(\\ell\\) points is covered by a vertical line.\n\nThe remaining \\(m-c\\) lines are either horizontal (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)) or diagonal (\\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).  \nEach such line meets column \\(a\\) in **at most one** point:\n\n* a horizontal line \\(y=b\\) meets column \\(a\\) at the single point \\((a,b)\\) (if that point belongs to \\(T_n\\));\n* a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) meets column \\(a\\) at the single point \\((a,c-a)\\) (if that point belongs to \\(T_n\\)).\n\nConsequently the \\(m-c\\) non‑vertical lines can together cover at most \\(m-c\\) points of column \\(a\\).  \nSince all \\(\\ell\\) points of the column must be covered, we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\ell\\le m-c\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad n+1-a\\le m-c. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause the columns \\(1,\\dots ,a-1\\) are present, we have \\(c\\ge a-1\\).  \nSubstituting this lower bound for \\(c\\) into (5) yields\n\n\\[\nn+1-a\\le m-(a-1)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad n\\le m .\n\\]\n\nThis contradicts the assumption \\(m<n\\) in (4).  \nTherefore **any covering of \\(T_n\\) by non‑sunny lines needs at least \\(n\\) such lines**:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{minimum number of non‑sunny lines}=n}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Determination of the admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\nFrom (6) and the relation (2) we obtain\n\n\\[\nv+h+d\\ge n .\n\\]\n\nSince the total number of lines is exactly \\(n\\), equality must hold and the number of sunny lines is forced to be\n\n\\[\ns=n-(v+h+d)=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus **every admissible configuration contains no sunny line**; i.e. the only possible value of \\(k\\) is \\(0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nThe family of vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n\\,\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\) belonging to \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).  \nHence \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) satisfies the covering requirement, and it contains exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion\n\nThe lower‑bound argument shows that at least \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines are indispensable, and the explicit construction with the \\(n\\) vertical lines shows that this bound is attainable. Consequently the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
