            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a positive integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_n\\) is the integer lattice points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices\n\\((1,1),\\;(n,1),\\;(1,n)\\).  \nFor a line \\(L\\) we call it **non‑sunny** if it is parallel to one of the three lines  \n\n\\[\nx=\\text{const},\\qquad y=\\text{const},\\qquad x+y=\\text{const},\n\\]\n\nand **sunny** otherwise.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  A column‑covering lemma  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 2.1.}\\qquad\n\\text{If }L\\text{ is not vertical, then for each }i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\text{ the set }L\\cap\\{x=i\\}\\cap T_n\\text{ contains at most one point.}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(L\\) is horizontal, it has equation \\(y=c\\); intersecting with the vertical line\n\\(x=i\\) yields the single point \\((i,c)\\) (if it belongs to \\(T_n\\)).  \nIf \\(L\\) is diagonal, it has equation \\(x+y=c\\); intersecting with \\(x=i\\) gives the\nsingle point \\((i,c-i)\\).  \nIf \\(L\\) is sunny, it can be written \\(y=mx+q\\) with \\(m\\neq0,-1\\); substituting\n\\(x=i\\) again yields a unique point \\((i,mi+q)\\).  ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Description of any covering with \\(n\\) lines  \n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of **\\(n\\) distinct lines** covers every point of\n\\(T_n\\).\n\n#### 3.1  Vertical columns  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nV=\\{\\,i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\mid x=i\\text{ belongs to }\\mathcal L\\,\\},\n\\qquad v=|V|.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\in V\\) the whole column \\(\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}\\) is already covered\nby the vertical line \\(x=i\\).\n\nDenote by  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus V\n\\]\n\nthe set of **uncovered columns**.  \nIf \\(U=\\varnothing\\) then \\(v=n\\) and \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists solely of the \\(n\\) vertical\nlines; this is a valid configuration and contains no sunny line.  \nHenceforth suppose \\(U\\neq\\varnothing\\) and let  \n\n\\[\ns=\\min U .\n\\]\n\nThus the columns \\(1,2,\\dots ,s-1\\) are covered by vertical lines and the columns\n\\(s,s+1,\\dots ,n\\) are not.\n\nFor a column \\(i\\in U\\) the number of points that have to be covered is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i=n+1-i .\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma 2.1 each **non‑vertical** line can contribute at most one point to column \\(i\\);\nconsequently at least \\(\\ell_i\\) distinct non‑vertical lines are needed to cover column\n\\(i\\).  Since the same line may serve several columns, the *only* lower bound that\nfollows from Lemma 2.1 is\n\n\\[\n\\text{# non‑vertical lines}\\;\\ge\\;\\max_{i\\in U}\\ell_i\n      =\\ell_s=n+1-s .\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, we have\n\n\\[\nn\\;=\\;v+\\bigl(\\text{# non‑vertical lines}\\bigr)\n   \\;\\ge\\; (s-1)+(n+1-s)=n .\n\\]\n\nAll inequalities are therefore equalities.  In particular\n\n\\[\nv=s-1,\\qquad\\text{# non‑vertical lines}=n+1-s,\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nand **every** column \\(i\\in U\\) is covered by exactly one point of each\nnon‑vertical line.  Hence each non‑vertical line meets every column\n\\(i\\in U\\) in exactly one point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n#### 3.2  Shape of the non‑vertical lines  \n\nFix a non‑vertical line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\).  For each \\(i\\in U\\) write the unique\nintersection point as \\((i,\\,y_i)\\) with \\(1\\le y_i\\le n+1-i\\).  Because the points\n\\((i,y_i)\\) lie on a straight line, there exist real numbers \\(m,b\\) such that  \n\n\\[\ny_i=m\\,i+b\\qquad\\text{for all }i\\in U .\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\nSince the right‑hand side must be an integer for every integer \\(i\\in U\\), the slope\n\\(m\\) is rational.  Moreover the admissible interval for \\(y_i\\) shrinks by exactly\n\\(1\\) when \\(i\\) increases by \\(1\\) (the upper bound is \\(n+1-i\\)).  Consequently the\ndifference  \n\n\\[\ny_{i+1}-y_i = m\n\\]\n\nmust be **exactly \\(-1\\)**; any other value would eventually violate the bound\n\\(y_i\\ge 1\\) or \\(y_i\\le n+1-i\\) for some \\(i\\in U\\).  Hence \\(m=-1\\) and (3.3) becomes  \n\n\\[\ny_i = -i + c\\qquad(c\\in\\mathbb Z).\n\\]\n\nThe constant \\(c\\) equals the sum \\(i+y_i\\); therefore the line \\(\\ell\\) is precisely the\ndiagonal line  \n\n\\[\nx+y=c .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\) meets the column \\(i=n\\) (the last column of \\(U\\)) at the point\n\\((n,1)\\), we have \\(c=n+1\\).  Varying \\(i\\) over \\(U=\\{s,\\dots ,n\\}\\) shows that the\npossible constants are  \n\n\\[\nc\\in\\{s+1,\\;s+2,\\;\\dots ,\\;n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus the set of non‑vertical lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,x+y=s+1,\\;x+y=s+2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x+y=n+1\\,\\}.\n\\tag{3.4}\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are **diagonal**, i.e. non‑sunny.\n\n#### 3.3  Final form of a covering  \n\nCombining (3.2) and (3.4) we obtain the complete description of any family of\n\\(n\\) lines covering \\(T_n\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\n\\mathcal L=\n\\bigl\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=s-1\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigl\\{\\,x+y=s+1,\\;x+y=s+2,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\,\\bigr\\},\n\\;}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(s\\) is an integer with \\(1\\le s\\le n+1\\).  Every line appearing in \\(\\mathcal L\\)\nis either vertical or diagonal, hence **non‑sunny**.  Consequently the number of sunny\nlines in any admissible configuration is\n\n\\[\nk=0 .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake \\(s=1\\) in the description above.  Then the family reduces to the \\(n\\) diagonal\nlines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\,x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;\\dots ,\\;x+y=n+1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) and therefore lies on\nthe line \\(x+y=a+b\\) belonging to \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).  The lines are distinct, none of\nthem is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence they are\nall non‑sunny.  Thus a covering with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists for every\n\\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n(Equivalently, one may choose \\(s=n+1\\) and obtain the family of the \\(n\\) vertical\nlines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).)\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nEvery covering of the set \\(T_n\\) by exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines must be of the form\ndescribed in §3 and therefore contains **no sunny line**.  The construction in §4\nshows that such a covering indeed exists.  Hence the set of admissible values of\n\\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k=0\\,}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
