            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_n\\) consists of the lattice points inside the right‑angled triangle with\nvertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \nA line is called **non‑sunny** if it is parallel to one of the three lines  \n\n\\[\nx=\\text{const},\\qquad y=\\text{const},\\qquad x+y=\\text{const},\n\\]\n\nand **sunny** otherwise.\n\nFor a column index \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) write  \n\n\\[\nC_i=\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\},\n\\qquad h_i:=|C_i|=n+1-i .\n\\]\n\nThus column \\(i\\) contains exactly \\(h_i\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Column‑covering Lemma  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 2.1.}\\qquad\n\\text{If a line }L\\text{ is not vertical, then for each }i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\text{ the set }L\\cap C_i\\text{ contains at most one point.}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non‑vertical line can be written as \\(y=mx+q\\) with a finite slope \\(m\\).  \nIntersecting with the vertical line \\(x=i\\) gives the single point \\((i,mi+q)\\); if this\npoint lies in \\(T_n\\) it belongs to \\(C_i\\), otherwise the intersection is empty.\n∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Structure of a covering with \\(n\\) lines  \n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of **\\(n\\) distinct lines** covers every point of\n\\(T_n\\).\n\n#### 3.1  Vertical columns  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nV=\\{\\,i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\mid x=i\\in\\mathcal L\\,\\},\\qquad v=|V|.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\in V\\) the whole column \\(C_i\\) is covered by the vertical line\n\\(x=i\\).\n\nDenote the set of *uncovered* columns by  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus V .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(U=\\varnothing\\) then \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists solely of the \\(n\\) vertical lines,\nwhich is a valid configuration with \\(k=0\\).  \nHenceforth suppose \\(U\\neq\\varnothing\\) and let  \n\n\\[\ns=\\min U .\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered columns are exactly  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{s,s+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, let \\(i\\in U\\).  By Lemma 2.1 each non‑vertical line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) can\ncontribute at most one point to column \\(i\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\nh_i\\le n-v\\qquad\\text{for every }i\\in U .\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nSince the heights \\(h_i\\) are decreasing, the strongest inequality in (3.2) is for\n\\(i=s\\); i.e. \\(h_s=n+1-s\\le n-v\\).  Because the columns \\(1,\\dots ,s-1\\) are\nvertical, we have \\(v\\ge s-1\\).  Combining the two inequalities yields  \n\n\\[\ns-1\\le v\\le s-1,\n\\]\n\nhence \\(v=s-1\\) and (3.1) holds.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nr:=|U|=n-s+1=v .\n\\]\n\nThus exactly \\(r\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are **not vertical**; they must cover all\npoints of the sub‑triangle  \n\n\\[\nT_r=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le r+1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the set \\(\\bigcup_{i=s}^{n}C_i\\).  The points of \\(T_r\\) are\n\\(\\displaystyle\\frac{r(r+1)}2\\) in number, and the \\(r\\) non‑vertical lines are\nexactly enough to cover them (the same situation as for the original problem but\nwith the smaller parameter \\(r\\)).\n\nConsequently every admissible configuration for the size \\(n\\) consists of  \n\n* the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=s-1\\) (all non‑sunny), and  \n* a covering of the triangle \\(T_r\\) with the remaining \\(r\\) lines.\n\nIn particular, the number of sunny lines in a configuration for \\(n\\) equals the\nnumber of sunny lines in the induced configuration for the smaller size \\(r\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Induction on \\(n\\)\n\nWe prove by induction that for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible values\nof \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n#### Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe triangle \\(T_3\\) contains the points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n\\]\n\n*Configuration with \\(k=0\\).*  \nThe three vertical lines \\(x=1,2,3\\) cover all six points; none of them is sunny.\n\n*Configuration with \\(k=1\\).*  \nTake the vertical lines \\(x=1,2\\) and the line through \\((3,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\);\nits slope is \\(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\neq0,-1\\), so it is sunny.  Every point of \\(T_3\\) is\ncovered.  \n\nA direct inspection shows that no covering with two sunny lines exists (any two\nsunny lines intersect each column in at most two points, but column 1 contains\nthree points).  Hence for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n#### Inductive step  \n\nAssume the statement holds for all integers \\(m\\) with \\(3\\le m<n\\).  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a covering of \\(T_n\\) with \\(n\\) lines and let \\(k\\) be the\nnumber of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nIf all lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are vertical, then \\(k=0\\).  \nOtherwise, by the analysis of Section 3 the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) splits into\n\n* vertical lines covering the first \\(s-1\\) columns, and  \n* a covering of the smaller triangle \\(T_r\\) (\\(r=n-s+1<n\\)) with the remaining\n  \\(r\\) lines.\n\nThe sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are exactly the sunny lines of the covering of\n\\(T_r\\); therefore \\(k\\) equals the number of sunny lines in a covering of size\n\\(r\\).  By the induction hypothesis this number can only be \\(0\\) or \\(1\\).\n\nThus for the original size \\(n\\) we also have \\(k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\).  No larger value of\n\\(k\\) can occur.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Existence for the two admissible values  \n\n*\\(k=0\\).*  \n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\,\\}\n\\]\nis a family of \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines covering every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  \nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and add the line through the\npoints \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\).  Its slope is \\(-\\dfrac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}\\neq0,-1\\); hence\nit is sunny.  The vertical lines cover all points of columns \\(1,\\dots ,n-1\\); the\nremaining point \\((n,1)\\) lies on the sunny line, so every point of \\(T_n\\) is\ncovered.  Hence a configuration with exactly one sunny line exists for every\n\\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the only non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there\nexist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\text{ or }k=1}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
